================================================================================
1. WHAT THE COMMIT DOES
================================================================================

It removes the K5a USB oracle from stage 9 and promotes it to its own stage 15.
The guest reads the same FAT32 image over qemu-xhci + usb-storage
(kernel/scripts/test-boot.sh:99-100), and emits three USBLIST blocks and
USBMCOPY blocks (kernel/src/xhciselftest.rs). Stage 15 then:

  - runs mdir -i on the same corpus image for ::/, ::/projects and
    ::/projects/2026 january notes, filters only mdir's headers, and diffs the
    text block by block against the guest's USBLIST output (scripts/check.sh,
    stage 15, ~lines 375-435);
  - runs mcopy -i ... - for each USBMCOPY target and diffs the guest's lines
    against the file's bytes on the host (~lines 440-480);
  - flips one byte in "plain 8.3 upper\n" in a copy of the corpus and asserts the
    mcopy comparison reports DIFFER (the sabotage gate, ~lines 482-528).

================================================================================
2. IS THE ORACLE INDEPENDENT OF THE KERNEL DRIVER?
================================================================================

Yes, on the reading side. mdir and mcopy are mtools; they parse the same image
with code that was written independently of this repository, and the values the
guest prints are computed from FAT directory entries in the image
(kernel/src/fat.rs list_dir_mdir, read_file), not from a constant. The guest's
listing text is chosen to look like mdir's, which is a formatting choice, not a
shared oracle: if the kernel mis-decodes a size, a date, an LFN or an entry
order, mdir's text differs.

One caveat, said plainly: the corpus image itself is built by this repository
(kernel/src/fat32_corpus.img, from tools/gen-fat32-corpus.py). mtools reading it
independently is what makes the comparison meaningful, and that is exactly the
"external tool as the oracle" pattern AGENTS.md rule 2 asks for.

================================================================================
3. DOES IT CLOSE THE K5a FINDING?
================================================================================

K5a F1 ("no check independent of the driver has ever run; a skip is a pass") and
F2 ("the listing is printed, never graded") are closed *as reporting*: stage 15
now runs in CI on every push and grades both the listing and the file bytes,
and CI treats any SKIP as a failure (check.sh's final block:
`if [ "$SKIPPED" -gt 0 ] && [ "${CI:-}" = "true" ]` -> exit 1).

They are NOT closed as enforcement. Finding F2.10-1 below is the reason, and it
is blocking.

In the last green run before this review the stage prints what it should:

  $ gh run view 41631557986 --log | grep -E "check 15/15|mdir-vs-usb|mcopy-vs-usb|SABOTAGE GATE"
