================================================================================
5. FINDING F2.10-1 (blocking): stage 15's failures never reach the exit code
================================================================================

scripts/check.sh wraps the whole of stage 15 in a pipeline:

  530:  rm -f "$MUT"
  531:    fi
  532:} 2>&1 | tee "$USB_OUT"

Everything the stage does, including every `FAILED=1`, runs in the right-hand
side of a pipe, i.e. in a subshell. Assignments there do not survive into the
parent shell, so the `FAILED` that line 536 tests is still 0. Plant G shows it
end to end: "FAIL: USBMCOPY vs mcopy mismatch" is printed, and the same run ends
"check: ALL PASSED" with a green conclusion.

Consequences:
  - a kernel that reads the wrong bytes over USB does not turn CI red;
  - a kernel that prints a wrong USB listing does not turn CI red through this
    stage (plant F only went red because stage 9's *module* comparison caught the
    same formatter independently — stage 9 is not piped);
  - the sabotage gate cannot fail CI either. If the oracle stops being sensitive
    ("SABOTAGE GATE" failing), that line is printed into a log and swallowed.
    This is precisely the decorative-check failure mode AGENTS.md section 3
    describes, one level below the check the commit added.

Fix direction (the author's, not mine): capture the block's status, e.g.
`{ ... } 2>&1 | tee "$USB_OUT"; [ "${PIPESTATUS[0]}" -eq 0 ] || FAILED=1`, or
write the log from a file and run the block in the parent shell. `pipefail` on
the whole script would also do it, and would be worth considering for the other
stages. No test edits are needed; the stage's own output is correct.

================================================================================
6. ONE TEST READ CLOSELY: the mcopy comparison
================================================================================

    def mcopy_blocks(path):   # guest's USBMCOPY blocks
        out, cur = {}, None
        for line in open(path, ...).read().splitlines():
            line = line.rstrip('\r')
            if line.startswith('USBMCOPY ') and line != 'USBMCOPY END':
                cur = line[9:].strip(); out[cur] = []
            elif line == 'USBMCOPY END': cur = None
            elif cur is not None: out[cur].append(line)
        return out

    for target, klines in k.items():
        proc = subprocess.run(['mcopy', '-i', corpus, target, '-'], ...)
        m_content = proc.stdout.decode('utf-8', 'replace').splitlines()
        if klines != m_content: ok = False

  Left: lines the guest printed after reading the file through the USB stack.
  Right: bytes mcopy reads out of the same image on the host.
  The left side is not a copy of the right side: the guest's values come from
  FAT chain reads over SCSI, and the right side from mtools parsing the image
  file. Neither side can be produced from the other except by a correct read.
  What the check cannot see: anything the guest prints outside a USBMCOPY block,
  and any file that no USBMCOPY line names (only two files are compared). It also
  compares text lines, so a byte difference that produces identical lines after
  `splitlines()` (e.g. a trailing-newline change) is invisible. Plant G was one
  character, visible; a covert change of a line terminator would not be.

